# Test fixtures are bytes, not source. Several are plain text that the
# tests compare against decoded output, so a checkout that rewrote their
# line endings would fail the comparison on Windows and nowhere else.
**/testdata/** -text
# The same argument for the other fixture directory. crates/rgx-adapters-pdf
# keeps its real-world PDFs in `fixtures/`, and `core.autocrlf=true` turned
# reportlab.pdf from 1400 bytes into 1468 on a fresh checkout -- 68 CRs
# inserted into a PDF stream -- which fails `reportlab_real_file` on the
# machine that checked the code out and on nobody else's. The directory name
# is the convention; `-text` is the fix.
**/fixtures/** -text
# Fuzzing corpora are seed *inputs* to a decompressor, a container parser or
# a model loader, and are read byte for byte by the thing under test. The two
# tracked seeds here (seed-zip-eocd-tail, seed-safetensors-overflow) already
# contain NULs, which is the whole point of them, and a CRLF rewrite changes
# the offsets a parser is being tested against. Not `testdata/`, not
# `fixtures/`, and therefore unprotected until this line existed.
**/fuzz/corpus/** -text

# benchmark/diffrig/fx/ is the differential harness's fixture directory, and
# the fixtures are BYTES, not source. crlf.txt and crlf2.txt are CRLF on
# purpose -- that is the difference the harness exists to catch -- and bin.dat
# holds a NUL. autocrlf=true would rewrite the first pair and fail the
# comparison on Windows and nowhere else, and would corrupt the second.
/benchmark/diffrig/fx/** -text

# Pack stores and manifests are binary formats.
**/.rgx-tv/** -text

# Shell scripts have to reach bash as LF. autocrlf=true hands a Windows
# checkout every .sh as CRLF, and bash reads `set -o pipefail\r` as an
# invalid option name, so the documented release command (`bash
# scripts/release-all.sh`) died at its own `set` line. The scripts are
# source, not fixtures: they normalise, and the line endings are pinned
# here so the working tree cannot drift from the index again.
*.sh text eol=lf

